ion that would require an employee or a contractor to disclose\" a pupil's gender identity without the pupil's consent. I coded it as `sponsor`, with that section as the `provision_quote`.\n- **Senate floor vote (`ac492b92…`).** She voted Aye on the Senate's final passage of the amended text on 2024-06-13, and it passed 29-8. That is about 22% No, so the near-unanimous rule doesn't apply.\n\n**Why not rung 1.** Section 220.3 only says staff \"shall not be required to disclose\" without consent. It doesn't require them to keep the information from parents, so \"Require staff to keep a student's gender identity from parents unless the student consents\" isn't established.\n\n**Calls a reviewer might weigh:**\n- **Preemption.** I read the bill as `on-question`, not as a state law blocking local rules (which would be `adjacent`). Rung 2 is itself about forbidding disclosure rules, which is what this bill does.\n- **Single subject.** I treated the bill as single-subject. Besides the disclosure ban, it orders parent-support resources and bars retaliation against staff, but all of it concerns LGBTQ pupils' rights.\n- **One source.** Both passages are on one instrument, so this chair will compute as `single-source`.\n\nNo quotes were coded, because neither source has her own words, and `needs_source` is empty.","ttft_ms":15037,"type":"result","duration_ms":33138,"uuid":"ed947569-d0d7-4e98-ad88-48b7f7384d20","ttft_stream_ms":2064,"time_to_request_ms":494,"first_content_frame_ms":2066,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
